{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"II and III are true: sine reaches -1 at x=-\pi/2+2n\pi and 1 at x=\pi/2+2n\pi. I is false as a description of all zeros because x=2n\pi omits odd multiples of \pi. The captured worked solution explicitly agrees. No choice tokens or learner grade are supplied.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"selection","correct_value":"II and III only","correct_option":null,"value_type":"text","wrong_value":"I only","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"II and III only","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"- Statement I is false. The zeros occur at $x=0, \pm \pi, \pm 2\pi,\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\pi.$

- Statement II is true. The function has a minimum at $x=-\dfrac{\pi}{2},$ and the period is $2\pi.$ Therefore, the minimum values occur at $-\dfrac{\pi}{2}+2n\pi.$

- Statement III is true. The function has a maximum at $x=\dfrac{\pi}{2},$ and the period is $2\pi.$ Therefore, the maximum values occur at $\dfrac{\pi}{2}+2n\pi.$","disagrees":false}]}
